# opinions and notes

django is very much 2000-era piece of software
it works great with heirarchical inheritance and not great with traits or mixins (around models, it gets wordy)
putting classnames in text to get around circular inheritance is a neat workaround but still a workaround
pylance is a static analysis and doesn't understand dynamically generated variables and fns that django models have
mypy is a static anlysis that can, with django-stubs
chatgpt didn't know that that problem was coming, and initially recommended pylance without caveats
uv doesn't seem much better than anything that came before it, but it is nice to not have to run venv all the time
ruff is for formatting and removing unused imports
this is a lot of workarounds for using a dynamic type checking language
should've just used go
i think all i want in life is go with native algebraic data types
instal django-extensions to see a list of urls
i miss Result<T, E>. it should be required by law

# uv notes

brew install uv

uv to manage python versions, and dependencies, and run code

don't touch pip or venv

```
uv add DEPENDENCY
uv add --dev DEPENDENCY
uv remove DEPENDENCY
uv run pytest

uv run python manage.py runserver

uv run python -m django --version
uv run python manage.py shell
uv run python manage.py check

uv run python manage.py makemigrations APPNAME
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python manage.py test polls

uv run pytest
uv run python manage.py test

# update the dynamic model type checking
uv run mypy books

# check stuff
uv run mypy books              # Type errors
uv run ruff check books        # Lint errors
uv run python manage.py check  # Django configuration/model errors
```

when cloning this repo, initially run `uv sync`

# vs code notes

use ruff to auto-format on save, and auto-sort imports

Cmd-Shift-M to open "problems" panel

# other book social site notes

- **bookwyrm** too slow
- **hardcover** doesn't have leo the late bloomer, takes too many steps to mark a book as read, their top lists isn't personalized, missing young zeus
- **fable** is built to be an app, really app based, does have a nice way to start noting books youve read, where selecing one opens a fields of similar books, but it's strictly an app
- **goodreads** is amazon and abandoned

# data sources

bulk data download options:

 - open library - main source, contributor-run
 - wikidata - CC0, https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityData/Q892.json
 - viaf - ODC-BY
 - library of congress name authority, https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79005673.json
 - bookbrainz - CC0 and CC BY-SA, https://api.bookbrainz.org/1/author/4e8f8704-7c34-4a00-8ce5-f02bf26ca7df
 - marc - lots of structured fields, used by open library
 - open book genome project - no listed license, things like: readability scores, genres, moods, etc

queryable, no bulk download:

 - librevox - audiorecordings of public domain books, https://librivox.org/api/feed/authors/?id=1536&format=json

not appropriate for this project:

 - goodreads - no api keys, no licensing
 - librarything - no building competing datasets
 - storygraph - no api
 - isbdb - restrictive license, requires active subscription
 - google - cannot build databases, only live integration and caching

# coding ideas

i need to decide how i want to deal with all the classes that only exist to be serialized. doing asdf.__dict__ seems wonky, but maybe that's actually the best choice

i need to decide how i want to handle abstract classes and object parameters

future tables around interacting with a work

 - user - 
 - user_rating - 
 - conversation - an abstract place where a single conversation happens
 - conversation_to_work - each conversation can be part of multiple works
 - conversation_to_series - each conversation can be part of multiple series
 - message - on a conversation, some text and a user, can mark it as a review, otherwise a message is limited to a smaller size (reviews can be larger)
 - user_read
 - user_want_to_read - includes who recommended it, maybe a link to a message or a user
 - update - an update a user makes on their wall

each provenance record should have a state "IMPORTED" and "PROCESSED"
everytime it's imported maybe with different data, it's set to IMPORTED
everytime it's processed, it's set to PROCESSED
which means we can get a "processes all unprocessed records" api endpoint

for ML, look at https://skeptric.com/book-ner-work-of-art/
maybe train a classifier to extract author names from various text fields?
maybe just look for all name-looking things as a "possible list"

# ideas

we have three types of stuff: authors, editions, works
these are my three main "catalog types"
a work is an intellectual copyrightable piece of art
an edition is a published thing of a work
(i use book to mean edition, a lot)

any work had to have an initially published edition

this website focuses on works
(though a person can decide if they've read a specific edition of that work)
conversations are on works

technically each translation is a new work
we don't go that far
for our needs, a translation is a published edition of a work
(one source calls those things a "manifestation")

other manifestations are abridgements, and annotated
how do we note that? little tags?

groups
a group could be like "translations of a work"
a group could be an ordered series like "harry potter"
work can be in multiple groups
these are not user created

any review is specific for an edition, but is displayed on the work

conversations
conversation is a specific thing, separate from anything
but it can be shown on a page, and often is connected to a work, to a series, to the original untranslated work
this way we can just "attach" the same conversation to multiple "translations" of a book
or a whole series
sometimes a conversation is on a work, sometimes it's on a translation?
visually, in the conversation, there's a little notes which say which edition a person has read

easy way to add books and rate books, like old school netflix, hover over and quickly say "read, 0-3 stars, want to read"

is this too cute?
"originally written" is on the work
"originally published" in on the edition

when looking up books, multiple editions of a book, with different introductions, should be under one book entry

a pet
you choose the base form
it evolves as you read books
evolves based on genre
can give it hats on holidays
it is unique to you and cannot be given away
a new pet is made every year
immutable
can put older hats on newer pets
but hats are one time things, nonfungible
your animal can be put into fireplace mode

Take a photo to log a book or books as owned or read
get a book info from a picture

A good test of the book collator is if it can recognize the various Martin Fierro El Gaucho as the same work
lots of sites have trouble with that

a personal canon, your own top 8. like the myspace 8 favorite.

add soft delete to proveance_import, which makes all it's import data unusable for future consolidation
unnecessary right now

integrate with internet archive for lendable books, open downloads for public domain books, librevox for public-domain audiobooks, the user's local library for lendab lendable books, libby for lendable ebooks. then link to to paid services for audiobooks, kindle ebooks, amazon, whatever

keep an internal calendar of every day, mapped to a list of
- author birthdays
- iterary events
- current festivals beginning
- book publishing anniversaries
and only show to each user stuff they'd definitely care about, and stuff they might care about

outgoing user stream
each user can subscribe to their own stream
which is a feed of all things did, with a full content
and all things their following people did, with partial content
(because: people own their own shit, and can use it anywhere, but not other people's)

catalog page should credit where the data came from, e.g. openlibray, isfdb

# useful commands

find any books in a gzip file that contain the ids in ids.txt
get only the JSON
format it into one giant list so that jq can format it correctly

```
rg -z -Ff works_ids.txt ol_dump_editions_2026-10-31.txt.gz | cut -f5 | perl -nE'BEGIN{say "["} END{say "]"} say $_ . ","' >editions.json
```

then to look for all values of a certain json attribute

```
» jq '.[].by_statement | values' editions.json
```

```
http get localhost:8000/books/api/imports/files

http post localhost:8000/books/api/imports/files/works.txt.gz/import
http post localhost:8000/books/api/imports/files/authors.txt.gz/import
http post localhost:8000/books/api/imports/files/editions.txt.gz/import

http get localhost:8000/books/api/process/stats

http put localhost:8000/books/api/process/process/import/1
http put localhost:8000/books/api/process/process/import/2
http put localhost:8000/books/api/process/process/import/3

http get localhost:8000/books/api/process/stats

http put localhost:8000/books/api/identitymapping/authors 
```

notify mac at the end of a long running command, only for iterm2
`your-long-command; printf '\e]9;Command finished!\a'`

get list of keys
`jq -r 'keys[]' file.json`
